C = [
("h1", "17. Objections and Replies"),
("p", "This chapter takes the strongest objections to the position and answers each from Scripture. The objections are stated as their holders state them. Three modern writers are named, each from a free full text the author has read through: Thomas L. Constable, in his notes on Philippians, Colossians, 1 Timothy and Titus; John Piper, in his sermon on Colossians 1:13–20; and N. T. Wright, in his essay on Jesus and the identity of God. Each is quoted only from those texts. Each fortifies the thesis at some points and holds a different view at others, and the chapter says which is which. Where an objection is held by a tradition and none of the three was read on it, it is stated in the volume’s words, and no sentence is placed in any person’s mouth."),
("sub", "17.1 Objection: Paul’s Christ is the last Adam and no more"),
("p", "This reading holds that Paul’s sayings about Christ are about a man, the ideal man who succeeds where Adam failed. Wright describes the proposal of James Dunn in these terms: that Paul in Philippians 2 and similar passages did not state a high view of Christ but “an Adam-christology.” The volume names the objection as Wright states it, and does not quote Dunn, whose book the author has not read."),
("p", "The volume replies that the Adam parallel is real, and that Paul draws it. The first volume of the series read Romans 5 and 1 Corinthians 15 for it. But the sayings about creation do not fit a man alone. “For by him were all things created, that are in heaven, and that are in earth” (Colossians 1:16) cannot be said of Adam, nor of any man. “And he is before all things” (Colossians 1:17) is not said of Adam. Paul himself marks the difference in 1 Corinthians 15:47: “The first man is of the earth, earthy: the second man is the Lord from heaven.” The second man is Lord from heaven. Wright, for his part, does not accept the Adam reading as the whole: he holds that Philippians 2 draws on the “fiercely monotheistic theology of Isaiah 40-55,” and so agrees with the volume that the hymn says more than a comparison with Adam."),
("sub", "17.2 Objection: “firstborn” means first created"),
("p", "This is the objection of a long tradition that read the title in Colossians 1:15 as placing Christ among the creatures, the first of them. No writer is named here, because none was read for this volume, and the objection is stated in the volume’s words."),
("p", "The volume replies from the verse that follows. The title is given with a reason: “For by him were all things created” (Colossians 1:16). A creature among the creatures could not be said to have created all things. Constable states the point: “What firstborn does not mean is that Christ was the first created being.” Piper gives four reasons and the second is the same as the volume’s: the ground given in the next verse contradicts the claim that he is part of creation. The three agree. The objection is answered from the text, and the answer does not need the later creeds."),
("sub", "17.3 Objection: he emptied himself of divine attributes"),
("p", "This is the objection of those who read Philippians 2:7 as a list: that in becoming man he laid aside knowledge, power or glory. It is held by a tradition and is stated in the volume’s words, and no writer is credited with it."),
("p", "The volume replies that the verse names no attribute. Constable writes, “Paul did not say that Jesus emptied Himself of some thing. He simply said that He emptied Himself,” and, “Jesus did not cease to be God when He became a man.” Wright objects to the question as it is usually asked, holding that it assumes “a quite unbiblical view of God.” The three do not agree on the reason, and the volume follows Constable on the grammar: the verse names an act, followed by a taking, and lists nothing."),
("sub", "17.4 Objection: the sayings should be read as Israel’s story, not as a doctrine of nature"),
("p", "This is Wright’s method, and it is directed at the volume’s approach. He holds that Paul and the other writers speak of Jesus within the story of Israel’s God, and that one finds in them “incipient trinitarian theology without needing to use any of the technical terms.” He holds that the sayings of Paul are best read as the identity of the one God, shown in Jesus, and he calls 1 Corinthians 8:6 “possibly the single most revolutionary christological formulation in the whole of early Christianity.”"),
("p", "The volume agrees with a good deal of this. It agrees that Paul needs no technical terms, and it agrees that the sayings place Christ on the side of the one God. It differs in two ways. First, it does not name what the sayings contain as a theology of the Trinity, since the passages do not use the word and name only some of the relations. Second, it does not read the story of Israel into the sayings of Colossians and Philippians as Wright does. They are written to a Body, and not to Israel, and the volume reads the story of Israel only where the passage itself places it, as in Romans 9. The disagreement is one of method, and the sentences of Paul are the same in both."),
("sub", "17.5 Objection: “son of God” is Israel’s title and Christ takes it up"),
("p", "Wright also holds that the title “Son of God” was used of Israel (Exodus 4:22), of the king (Psalm 89:27) and of the Messiah, and that it sums up Israel’s destiny in Jesus. The volume reads the passages as stated, and agrees that Exodus 4:22 and Psalm 89:27 give the title to Israel and to the king. It holds that Paul’s sayings in Colossians 1:15 and Romans 8:29 give the title of firstborn to Christ in a use of their own, and that no sonship passes to the Body from Israel’s title. The standing sentence of the series applies: the Body receives blessing through Christ alone, and not through participation in Israel’s covenants. Wright’s line joins Israel and Christ more closely than the volume does, and the volume says so."),
("sub", "17.6 Objection: Titus 2:13 names two persons"),
("p", "This is an objection about the punctuation and the sense of the verse, held by a tradition, and none of the three writers holds it. The volume replies as in chapter 11. Constable reads the verse as naming one person: “Paul described the appearing and glory of one Person: our great God and Savior, Christ Jesus.” The volume agrees, and notes that the thesis does not rest on this verse."),
("sub", "17.7 Objection: the high sayings are borrowed hymns, not Paul’s teaching"),
("p", "This objection holds that Philippians 2 and 1 Timothy 3:16 are fragments Paul took over, and that they do not show what he himself taught. Constable states the possibility in each case: of 1 Timothy 3:16, “Paul evidently was quoting a fragment of a hymn, or a statement of the apostolic church.”"),
("p", "The volume replies that Paul’s use of a hymn is his teaching, since he sets it in his letter and builds his appeal on it. And Colossians 2:9 is no borrowed fragment. It stands in Paul’s own argument against the rival teachers, and he writes it in the first person: “Beware lest any man spoil you.” Whether a saying was first sung or first written, Paul gives it his name by using it."),
("sub", "17.8 Objection: Piper presses the sayings further than Paul does"),
("p", "Piper reads Colossians 1:19 and 2:9 as plain statements that Christ is God: “He is fully God.” He adds that the image is the visible God, and that Christ is the creator of all that is not God. He goes on to say that Christ holds the world together moment by moment, and that all things exist to display his glory. The volume agrees with the first statements, which come from the sentences. It does not adopt the second pair as part of its thesis, since the sentences speak of “consist” and “for him” without Piper’s further account of why. The disagreement is one of extent, and the volume marks it."),
("ctbl", "The objections at a glance.", [
    ("Objection", "Who holds it, as read", "The reply in one line", "Where answered"),
    ("Paul’s Christ is the last Adam and no more", "James Dunn, as Wright describes his book; Wright disagrees", "Colossians 1:16, 17 and 1 Corinthians 15:47 say more than a comparison with Adam", "17.1; chapters 4, 7"),
    ("“Firstborn” means first created", "A tradition; Constable and Piper reject it", "The reason is given in the next verse: by him all things were created", "17.2; chapter 6"),
    ("He emptied himself of divine attributes", "A tradition; Constable says the verse lists none; Wright objects to the question", "The verse names an act and a taking, not an attribute", "17.3; chapter 4"),
    ("The sayings are Israel’s story, not a doctrine of nature", "N. T. Wright", "The sayings are agreed in substance; the method differs", "17.4; chapters 1, 15"),
    ("“Son of God” is Israel’s title taken up by Christ", "N. T. Wright", "The uses of firstborn stay apart; no sonship passes to the Body", "17.5; chapter 6"),
    ("Titus 2:13 names two persons", "A tradition; Constable reads one person", "One article, one appearing, one subject of “gave himself”", "17.6; chapter 11"),
    ("The high sayings are borrowed hymns", "Constable suggests a hymn fragment in 1 Timothy 3:16", "Paul adopts what he uses; Colossians 2:9 is his own argument", "17.7; chapters 8, 10"),
    ("Piper presses the sayings further than Paul does", "John Piper", "Agreed for the sayings; his further account is not adopted", "17.8; chapters 7, 8"),
  ], [0.26, 0.26, 0.30, 0.18]),
]
